---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ListToColumnTool from '../../components/ListToColumnTool.astro';
import { toolBySlug } from '../../data/tools';
import { listToColumn } from '../../lib/list-convert';

const tool = toolBySlug('comma-separated-list-to-column');

// Worked examples come from the real engine at build time.
const sample = '["red", "orange", "yellow", "green"]';
const sampleOut = listToColumn(sample).output;
const quotedCsv = listToColumn('"Smith, John", "Doe, Jane"');
const mixedSeparators = listToColumn('a;b;c;d,e').output.split('\n');
const trailing = listToColumn('a, b, c,');

const steps = [
  'Paste the list. Comma, semicolon, pipe, tab and new-line separated text is detected automatically; the note under the Separator menu says which one was found.',
  'If detection picked the wrong one, choose the separator yourself, or <em>Custom…</em> for anything else, such as <code>--</code> or <code> :: </code>.',
  'Leave <strong>Remove surrounding quotes</strong> on to strip <code>\'…\'</code>, <code>"…"</code> and <code>`…`</code> from each item; tick <strong>Remove duplicates</strong> or pick a <strong>Sort</strong> if you need them.',
  '<strong>Copy to clipboard</strong>, <strong>Download .txt</strong>, or <strong>Join into a list →</strong> to send the column to the reverse tool.',
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How does automatic separator detection work?',
    a: 'The tool counts how often each common separator (comma, semicolon, pipe, tab, new line) appears in your text and splits on the most frequent one. If none appear it falls back to splitting on spaces. You can always override it from the Separator menu.',
  },
  {
    q: 'My values contain commas inside quotes. Will they be split incorrectly?',
    a: 'Yes — this tool performs a simple split and does not parse quoted CSV fields, so a value like <code>"Smith, John"</code> is broken into two items. For that kind of data, choose a separator that does not appear inside the values, or export tab-separated from the spreadsheet first.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I paste a JSON or Python array directly?',
    a: 'Yes. Surrounding square brackets, parentheses or braces are removed automatically, and the “Remove surrounding quotes” option strips the quote marks from each element, leaving you with bare values.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is anything stored or sent to a server?',
    a: 'No. The page is static and the conversion runs in your browser. Your data never leaves your device.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout {tool} {steps} {faq}>
  <ListToColumnTool />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>When you need a column instead of a list</h2>
    <p>
      Data tends to travel as a single line — a comma separated field in a CRM export, a JSON array in an API response, a
      semicolon separated recipient list in an email header. But spreadsheets, form fields and many scripts want each value on
      its own row. This tool makes that switch without opening a text editor and doing a find-and-replace, and it strips the
      brackets and quotes that a JSON array, Python list or SQL <code>IN (…)</code> clause wraps around the values.
    </p>

    <h3>Paste into a spreadsheet column</h3>
    <p>
      Convert the list, copy the result, click a cell in Excel or Google Sheets and paste. Because each item is on its own line,
      the spreadsheet fills one cell per row automatically.
    </p>

    <h3>Read an ID list out of a query</h3>
    <p>
      Copy the contents of an <code>IN (…)</code> clause, paste it here, and the parentheses and quotes are stripped so you are
      left with the raw IDs — ready for a lookup or a VLOOKUP.
    </p>

    <h3>Count and de-duplicate</h3>
    <p>
      The item counter shows how many values you actually have. Tick <em>Remove duplicates</em> and the counter shows how many
      were dropped — a quick sanity check on messy exports. For a column that is already one-per-line, the dedicated
      <a href="/tools/remove-duplicate-lines">Remove Duplicate Lines</a> tool adds case-insensitive matching and keep-last.
    </p>

    <h2>Example</h2>
    <p>A JSON array pasted whole:</p>
    <pre><code>{sample}</code></pre>
    <p>comes out as one bare value per line — the brackets and quotes are removed and the comma is detected as the separator:</p>
    <pre><code>{sampleOut}</code></pre>

    <h2>Options explained</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Separator: Detect automatically</strong> counts commas, semicolons, pipes, tabs and new lines and splits on the most frequent one; on a tie the earlier in that list wins (comma first). If none appears at all, the text is split on whitespace.</li>
      <li><strong>Comma / Semicolon / Pipe / Tab / Any whitespace / Custom…</strong> force a separator. <em>Any whitespace</em> splits on runs of spaces, tabs and newlines together. <em>Custom…</em> uses exactly the characters you type, spaces included; leaving the custom box empty falls back to automatic detection.</li>
      <li><strong>Trim spaces around items</strong> — <code>a , b</code> gives <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>, not <code>a </code> and <code> b</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Skip empty items</strong> — <code>a,,b</code> gives two items and reports one dropped. Untick to keep an empty line in the output.</li>
      <li><strong>Remove surrounding quotes</strong> strips one matching pair of <code>'</code>, <code>"</code> or <code>`</code> from each item; a quote on one side only is left alone.</li>
      <li><strong>Remove duplicates</strong> matches exact text, so <code>a</code> and <code>A</code> are different items.</li>
      <li><strong>Reverse order</strong>, <strong>Sort</strong> and <strong>Letter case</strong> behave as in the <a href="/tools/column-to-comma-separated-list">column to list</a> tool: natural-order A → Z, numeric sorts that read the first number in each item, and simple case changes.</li>
      <li>Surrounding <code>[ ]</code>, <code>( )</code> or <code>{'{ }'}</code> around the whole input are removed before splitting, so a JSON array, a Python tuple or an SQL <code>IN (…)</code> clause can be pasted as-is.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Edge cases and common errors</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Quoted CSV fields containing the separator are split anyway.</strong> <code>"Smith, John", "Doe, Jane"</code> becomes {quotedCsv.count} items, not two. The tool does not parse CSV quoting; use a separator that is not present in the values, or export from the spreadsheet with tabs.</li>
      <li><strong>Nested arrays lose only the outer brackets.</strong> <code>[["a","b"],["c"]]</code> gives <code>["a"</code>, <code>"b"]</code> and <code>["c"]</code>. Flatten the structure first, or use the <a href="/tools/json-formatter">JSON Formatter</a> to reshape it.</li>
      <li><strong>Mixed separators go to the majority.</strong> <code>a;b;c;d,e</code> has three semicolons and one comma, so it splits on semicolons and <code>{mixedSeparators.at(-1)}</code> stays one item. Pick the separator manually if the data is inconsistent.</li>
      <li><strong>A trailing separator</strong> such as <code>a, b, c,</code> produces {trailing.count} items and {trailing.dropped} dropped, not an error.</li>
      <li><strong>Spaces are only a separator as a last resort.</strong> <code>a b c</code> with no other separator gives three items, but <code>a b, c</code> gives <code>a b</code> and <code>c</code>, because the comma wins.</li>
      <li><strong>An unbalanced quote</strong> (<code>"a, b</code>) is kept: quote stripping needs a matching pair on both ends of an item.</li>
      <li><strong>Empty input</strong>, or just <code>[]</code>, gives an empty result and a count of 0.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Do the same job in a spreadsheet or in code</h2>
    <h3>Excel and Google Sheets</h3>
    <p>Both applications can split text to columns from the Data menu. To split into <em>rows</em> instead, use a formula:</p>
    <pre><code>=TRANSPOSE(SPLIT(A1, ","))          Google Sheets
=TEXTSPLIT(A1, , ",")               Excel 365</code></pre>

    <h3>Python</h3>
    <pre><code>items = [s.strip().strip('"\'') for s in text.split(",") if s.strip()]
print("\n".join(items))</code></pre>

    <h3>Command line</h3>
    <pre><code>tr ',' '\n' &lt; list.txt                   # Linux / macOS
(Get-Content list.txt) -split ','       # PowerShell</code></pre>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
